{"adrs":[{"kind":"adr","id":"0001","num":1,"slug":"use-a-kafka-protocol-log-for-domain-events","file":"docs/adr/0001-use-a-kafka-protocol-log-for-domain-events.md","mtime":1791098026331,"title":"Use a Kafka-protocol log for domain events","subtitle":"Services publish domain events to a Kafka-protocol log, keyed by aggregate id, encoded as protobuf.","status":"Proposed","statusKey":"proposed","date":"2026-10-01","author":"architect","owner":"","due":"","reopen":[],"applies":[],"tags":["kafka","messaging","events"],"components":["kafka","billing","orders"],"rel":{"depends_on":["0003"],"relates":["0002"]},"mentions":["0002"],"facts":{"decision":"Use a Kafka-protocol broker as the durable log between services. Key records by aggregate id to keep order. Encode payloads with protobuf. Ops may run Apache Kafka (KRaft) or Redpanda; both speak the same protocol.","question":null,"recommendation":null,"options":[{"panel":"D","title":null,"items":[{"key":"A","name":"Kafka protocol (Kafka or Redpanda)","topic":null,"verdict":{"kind":"ok","label":""},"verdictHead":"Verdict","fields":[{"label":"Pros","role":"pro","text":"Ordered partitions by key. Many consumer groups. Retention gives replay."},{"label":"Cons","role":"con","text":"One more stateful system to run."}]},{"key":"B","name":"NATS JetStream","topic":null,"verdict":{"kind":"warn","label":""},"verdictHead":"Verdict","fields":[{"label":"Pros","role":"pro","text":"Light to run. Single binary."},{"label":"Cons","role":"con","text":"Per-key order with parallel consumers needs manual subject sharding."}]},{"key":"C","name":"RabbitMQ","topic":null,"verdict":{"kind":"no","label":""},"verdictHead":"Verdict","fields":[{"label":"Pros","role":"pro","text":"Simple queues. Team knows it."},{"label":"Cons","role":"con","text":"No replay. Fan-out to new consumers needs new queues."}]},{"key":"D","name":"Database table as queue","topic":null,"verdict":{"kind":"no","label":""},"verdictHead":"Verdict","fields":[{"label":"Pros","role":"pro","text":"No new system."},{"label":"Cons","role":"con","text":"Ingress depends on the database. Polling cost grows with load."}]}]}],"questions":[],"consequences":["Positive: ingress stays up when the database is slow or down.","Positive: new consumers join with their own group and need no producer change.","Cost we accept: one more cluster to run and watch (lag, disk).","Cost we accept: partition count is hard to change without reordering keys.","Follow-ups: pkg/kafka module, deploy config, lag alerts."],"criteria":[]},"sections":[{"id":"A","title":"Decision"},{"id":"B","title":"Key numbers"},{"id":"C","title":"Context"},{"id":"D","title":"Options"},{"id":"E","title":"Scoring"},{"id":"F","title":"Where we stand"},{"id":"G","title":"Design"},{"id":"H","title":"Producer contract"},{"id":"I","title":"Risks"},{"id":"J","title":"Consequences"},{"id":"K","title":"Related"}],"text":"Decision span=3 Use a Kafka-protocol broker as the durable log between services. Key records by aggregate id to keep order. Encode payloads with protobuf. Ops may run Apache Kafka (KRaft) or Redpanda; both speak the same protocol. Key numbers span=3 500 msg/s Peak inbound assumption 32 Partitions per topic start value 7 d Raw retention replay window 3 Replication factor Context span=3 - Webhook handlers must reply fast, so a durable buffer must sit before the database. - Events in one aggregate must stay in order. - Several consumers read the same events: workflow, analytics, realtime push. - The database engine is still open (ADR-0002), so the log must not depend on it. Options span=3 Option Pros Cons Verdict --- --- --- --- A — Kafka protocol (Kafka or Redpanda) Ordered partitions by key. Many consumer groups. Retention gives replay. One more stateful system to run. ok B — NATS JetStream Light to run. Single binary. Per-key order with parallel consumers needs manual subject sharding. warn C — RabbitMQ Simple queues. Team knows it. No replay. Fan-out to new consumers needs new queues. no D — Database table as queue No new system. Ingress depends on the database. Polling cost grows with load. no Scoring span=3 Criterion weight Kafka NATS RabbitMQ DB table Ordering per key 3 5 3 2 4 Replay 3 5 4 1 2 Ops load 2 2 4 4 5 Team knowledge 1 3 2 4 5 Where we stand span=3 Simplicity \u003c- Scale 0.7 we pay ops cost now to avoid a migration later Build \u003c- Buy 0.8 managed or well-known OSS, no custom broker Design span=3 Ingress - (raw.v1) : key = account:chat (raw.v1) - Ingest: consumer group Ingest - (raw.dlq.v1) : poison records Relay - (events.v1) : key = aggregate id (events.v1) - Workflow \u0026 Analytics \u0026 Realtime Producer contract span=3 // Every record carries the same envelope; consumers deduplicate on EventID. type Envelope struct EventID string json:\"event id\" EventType string json:\"event type\" WorkspaceID string json:\"workspace id\" OccurredAt time.Time json:\"occurred at\" Payload byte json:\"payload\" // protobuf, see contract/proto func (p Producer) Publish(ctx context.Context, key string, e Envelope) error return p.client.ProduceSync(ctx, \u0026kgo.Record Topic: p.topic, Key: byte(key), Value: must(proto.Marshal(e)) ).FirstErr() syntax = \"proto3\"; package events.v1; message MessageReceived string conversation id = 1; int64 seq = 2; string text = 3; Risks span=3 Broker outage 2 5 relay spools to disk, ingress keeps accepting Hot partition 3 3 key by aggregate, watch lag per partition Schema drift 3 4 buf breaking-change check in CI Consequences span=3 - Positive: ingress stays up when the database is slow or down. - Positive: new consumers join with their own group and need no producer change. - Cost we accept: one more cluster to run and watch (lag, disk). - Cost we accept: partition count is hard to change without reordering keys. - Follow-ups: pkg/kafka module, deploy config, lag alerts. Related span=3 0002 the database choice decides whether option D is viable 0003 where pkg/kafka lives"},{"kind":"adr","id":"0002","num":2,"slug":"which-database-for-v1","file":"docs/adr/0002-which-database-for-v1.md","mtime":1791279245895,"title":"Which database for v1","subtitle":"PostgreSQL or MySQL for all module data","status":"Open","statusKey":"open","date":"2026-10-02","author":"architect","owner":"","due":"","reopen":[],"applies":[],"tags":["database","postgres"],"components":["orders-db","orders"],"rel":{},"mentions":[],"facts":{"decision":null,"question":"Which database engine stores all module data in v1? The choice fixes the migration tooling, the job-queue pattern and the managed offer we can buy.","recommendation":"PostgreSQL 16 for all module data. Revisit only if the host cannot sell a managed PostgreSQL with PITR.","options":[{"panel":"C","title":null,"items":[{"key":"A","name":"PostgreSQL 16","topic":null,"verdict":{"kind":"ok","label":""},"verdictHead":"Fit","fields":[{"label":"Pros","role":"pro","text":"SKIP LOCKED. Partial unique indexes. Declarative partitioning. JSONB."},{"label":"Cons","role":"con","text":"New to the team."},{"label":"Ops","role":"info","text":"Managed offer available."}]},{"key":"B","name":"MySQL 8","topic":null,"verdict":{"kind":"warn","label":""},"verdictHead":"Fit","fields":[{"label":"Pros","role":"pro","text":"Team knows it. Managed everywhere."},{"label":"Cons","role":"con","text":"No partial unique indexes."},{"label":"Ops","role":"info","text":"Managed offer available."}]},{"key":"C","name":"Both, per module","topic":null,"verdict":{"kind":"no","label":""},"verdictHead":"Fit","fields":[{"label":"Pros","role":"pro","text":"Each module picks."},{"label":"Cons","role":"con","text":"Two engines to back up, upgrade and monitor."},{"label":"Ops","role":"info","text":"High."}]}]}],"questions":["Does anyone on the team own database operations?","Is a managed PostgreSQL with PITR available on our host?"],"consequences":[],"criteria":["Ops load for the real team size.","Fit with the job-table and outbox patterns.","A managed offer with point-in-time recovery."]},"sections":[{"id":"A","title":"Question"},{"id":"B","title":"Context"},{"id":"C","title":"Options"},{"id":"D","title":"Evaluation criteria"},{"id":"E","title":"Recommendation"},{"id":"F","title":"Questions for the user"}],"text":"Question span=3 Which database engine stores all module data in v1? The choice fixes the migration tooling, the job-queue pattern and the managed offer we can buy. Context span=3 - Peak write load is about 500 rows per second (assumption). - Outbound sends use a job table with SELECT … FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED . - The team has run MySQL before; nobody has run PostgreSQL in production (U). Options span=3 Option Pros Cons Ops Fit --- --- --- --- --- A — PostgreSQL 16 SKIP LOCKED. Partial unique indexes. Declarative partitioning. JSONB. New to the team. Managed offer available. ok B — MySQL 8 Team knows it. Managed everywhere. No partial unique indexes. Managed offer available. warn C — Both, per module Each module picks. Two engines to back up, upgrade and monitor. High. no + Partial unique indexes solve \"unique when set\" keys + One engine for jobs, outbox and data - Team needs to learn vacuum and connection pooling ~ Managed PITR on the host (check retention) Evaluation criteria span=3 1. Ops load for the real team size. 2. Fit with the job-table and outbox patterns. 3. A managed offer with point-in-time recovery. Recommendation span=3 PostgreSQL 16 for all module data. Revisit only if the host cannot sell a managed PostgreSQL with PITR. -- the pattern that drives the choice SELECT id, payload FROM outbound job WHERE not before \u003c= now() ORDER BY id FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED LIMIT 50; Questions for the user span=3 1. Does anyone on the team own database operations? 2. Is a managed PostgreSQL with PITR available on our host?"},{"kind":"adr","id":"0003","num":3,"slug":"repository-layout","file":"docs/adr/0003-repository-layout.md","mtime":1791279245892,"title":"Repository layout","subtitle":"One Go module; services under services/, binaries under